Anencephaly is a neural tube defect . The neural tube is the embryonic structure that develops into the brain and spinal cord. It is formed between the 3 ^{rd} and 4 ^{th} weeks of gestation and defects of the tube can result in serious damage or death to the developing fetus. Anencephaly is a severe defect characterized by the absence of the brain, skull, and scalp.
In the early stages of anencephaly, the embryo is unable to produce the protein, Noggin. Noggin prevents the neural tube from closing and is necessary for proper development of the brain and spinal cord. In the absence of Noggin, the neural tube fails to close and does not develop normally. The embryo is unable to produce Noggin because it does not have the necessary genetic instruction for the production of Noggin. The fetus is missing brain tissue and the skull.
Anencephaly is the most severe neural tube defect and is one of the most common malformations of the central nervous system. It occurs in approximately 1 in 1,000 pregnancies. Anencephaly is four times more common than spina bifida and occurs with a higher frequency in males than in females.
Anencephaly can occur as a spontaneous (occurs without a known cause) or as a result of a genetic defect. Genetic defects are caused by abnormal genes that result in underproduction or overproduction of Noggin. This results in the failure to close the neural tube, which produces anencephaly.
The most common genetic cause of anencephaly is trisomy 13 (Patau syndrome) . This syndrome is caused by a misalignment of chromosomes during meiosis, which results in the formation of an extra 13 ^{th} chromosome. The extra chromosome causes the embryo to lack the necessary instructions for the production of Noggin, which results in anencephaly. Trisomy 13 is the most common cause of anencephaly and accounts for 25% of all cases. Other genetic defects that cause anencephaly include:
Trisomy 18 (Edwards syndrome);
Trisomy 21 (Down syndrome), sometimes referred to as nongenomic trisomy;
Trisomy 16 (Patau syndrome), which results in a duplication of the 16 ^{th} chromosome;
Trisomy 22 (Schnitzler-Karate syndrome);
Trisomy 7 (Trisomy 7 syndrome);
