Quantum mechanics is the name given to the branch of physics that describes the behavior of matter and energy at the atomic level, a field that is influenced by the peculiarities of the atomic system. We have already seen that quantum mechanics can be used to calculate the energy levels of atoms and molecules, and thus determine the spectra of atoms and molecules. In fact, the Schrödinger equation is the heart of quantum mechanics, and it is the key to understanding atomic and molecular spectra. In spite of its importance, the Schrödinger equation is a differential equation that is hard to solve exactly. Therefore, we must seek other ways to get the results we desire. One such way is to use a different type of equation, which we call the time-independent Schrödinger equation (TISE):
\begin{align*} \text{(3.6.1)} \hat{H}\psi=E\psi \end{align*}
where $H$ is the Hamiltonian, which depends on the nuclei and electrons, $\psi$ is the wavefunction, and $E$ is the energy. $H$ is a large, complicated equation, but we can reduce it to much simpler equations by making assumptions.
Assuming that the wavefunction is independent of time, we can derive an important result known as the time-independent Schrödinger equation (TISE). This equation tells us that the total energy ( $E$ ) of a quantum mechanical system is equal to the sum of all the energies of the individual components (nuclei and electrons), which we can write as
\begin{align*} \text{(3.6.2)} E_{s u m} = E_{n u c} + E_{e l} \end{align*}
The energy levels of a quantum mechanical system are determined by the energy levels of the individual components, so the TISE tells us that the energy levels of a quantum mechanical system are the sum of the energy levels of its components.
If we put the wavefunction into the Schrödinger equation, we can solve it for either the energy, $\psi$ , or the wavefunction, $\psi$ . The solution of the Schrödinger equation can be written as
\begin{align*} \text{(3.6.3)} \psi = A e^{- i E t / ℏ} \end{align*}
where $A$ is the normalization constant. Most of the time, we are interested in the energy, so the Schrödinger equation can be rewritten as
\begin{align*} \text{(3.6.4)} \hat{H}\psi = E \psi \end{align*}
This is the time-independent Schrödinger equation. Notice that this equation contains an energy that can be either positive or negative. The negative energy levels are not observable. The positive energy levels are the energy levels of the observable system. The negative energy levels are the energy levels of the unobservable system.
To solve the Schrödinger equation, we must make an assumption about the wavefunction. For example, we can assume that the wavefunction is a simple function of $r$ , $\theta$ , and $\phi$ . The wavefunction can be written as
\begin{align*} \text{(3.6.5)} \psi = N \left(\right. r , \theta , \phi \left.\right) \end{align*}
where $N$ is the normalization constant. If we put this wavefunction into the TISE, we get the following result:
\begin{align*} \text{(3.6.6)} H N \left(\right. r , \theta , \phi \left.\right) = E N \left(\right. r , \theta , \phi \left.\right) \end{align*}
This is an example of a partial differential equation (PDE). If we assume that the wavefunction is a function of $r$ alone, we can write the wavefunction as
\begin{align*} \text{(3.6.7)} \psi = N \left(\right. r \left.\right) \end{align*}
This PDE can be solved to give us the energy levels of the system. The wavefunction of the system is a function of $r$ alone, and the energy levels are a function of $r$ alone. Finally, we can assume that the wavefunction is a function of $r$ and $\theta$ alone. The wavefunction can be written as
\begin{align*} \text{(3.6.8)} \psi = N \left(\right. r , \theta \left.\right) \end{align*}
